Monitoring Cloud Infrastructure with Grafana
Objectives
Design and deploy a production-grade observability stack (Grafana + Prometheus + exporters) from a bare Ubuntu instance
Build multi-service Grafana dashboards that surface EC2-simulated, RDS-simulated, and host-level metrics using PromQL expressions
Implement threshold-based alerting with Alertmanager routing and verify end-to-end alert delivery
Prerequisites
Comfort with Linux system administration including systemd service management and file permissions
Familiarity with cloud infrastructure concepts: compute instances, managed databases, load balancers, and object storage
Understanding of time-series data concepts: scrape intervals, metric labels, and rate calculations
Lab Environment
You will work on a dedicated AWS EC2 Ubuntu instance provided by Al Nafi. The instance has a base Ubuntu installation; you will install all required tools in Task 1.

Task 1: Environment Setup â Install and Verify the Observability Stack
Step 1.1: Install System Dependencies, Docker, and Grafana
Your instance needs Docker (for running auxiliary containers), Grafana (the visualization layer), and Python 3 (for the metrics simulator you will write in Task 2). Install all three from their official repositories, not from Ubuntu's default apt index, so you receive current stable versions.

# Refresh package index and install transport utilities
sudo apt-get update -y
sudo apt-get install -y \
    ca-certificates \
    curl \
    gnupg \
    lsb-release \
    python3 \
    python3-pip \
    net-tools

# Official Docker installation guide: https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/ubuntu/
sudo install -m 0755 -d /etc/apt/keyrings
curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg \
    | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg
sudo chmod a+r /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg

sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list <<'EOF'
deb [arch=amd64 signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg] https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu jammy stable
EOF

sudo apt-get update -y
sudo apt-get install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io docker-compose-plugin
sudo usermod -aG docker "$USER"
Troubleshoot this step:

Error seen: E: Malformed entry 1 in list file /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list
Recovery: cat /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list â the file must be a single unbroken line with no backslashes; delete it with sudo rm /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list and re-run the tee heredoc block above.
Official docs: https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/ubuntu/
# Official Grafana installation guide: https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/installation/debian/
sudo mkdir -p /etc/apt/keyrings
curl -fsSL https://apt.grafana.com/gpg.key \
    | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/grafana.gpg

sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/grafana.list <<'EOF'
deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/grafana.gpg] https://apt.grafana.com stable main
EOF

sudo apt-get update -y
sudo apt-get install -y grafana
Troubleshoot this step:

Error seen: NO_PUBKEY or The following signatures couldn't be verified during apt-get update
Recovery: Confirm the keyring file exists and is non-empty: ls -lh /etc/apt/keyrings/grafana.gpg â if it is 0 bytes the curl command failed silently; re-run it with curl -fsSL https://apt.grafana.com/gpg.key | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/grafana.gpg and verify exit code 0.
Official docs: https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/installation/debian/
Step 1.2: Install Prometheus and Node Exporter as Systemd Services
Prometheus (the time-series metrics database) and Node Exporter (the agent that exposes host-level OS metrics) must each run as dedicated non-login system users and be managed by systemd so they survive reboots. Choose a stable release from the official Prometheus GitHub releases page; do not use Ubuntu's packaged version.

# Official Prometheus releases: https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/releases
PROM_VERSION="2.51.2"
cd /tmp
curl -fsSL "https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/releases/download/v${PROM_VERSION}/prometheus-${PROM_VERSION}.linux-amd64.tar.gz" \
    -o prometheus.tar.gz
tar -xzf prometheus.tar.gz

sudo useradd --no-create-home --shell /bin/false prometheus
sudo mkdir -p /etc/prometheus /var/lib/prometheus
sudo cp "prometheus-${PROM_VERSION}.linux-amd64/prometheus" /usr/local/bin/prometheus
sudo cp "prometheus-${PROM_VERSION}.linux-amd64/promtool"   /usr/local/bin/promtool
sudo cp -r "prometheus-${PROM_VERSION}.linux-amd64/consoles"          /etc/prometheus/
sudo cp -r "prometheus-${PROM_VERSION}.linux-amd64/console_libraries" /etc/prometheus/
sudo chown -R prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus /var/lib/prometheus \
    /usr/local/bin/prometheus /usr/local/bin/promtool
Troubleshoot this step:

Error seen: curl: (22) The requested URL returned error: 404 â the version string in the URL does not exist on GitHub.
Recovery: Visit https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/releases, copy the exact tag of the latest stable release, and replace PROM_VERSION in the command above before re-running.
Official docs: https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/installation/
# Official Node Exporter releases: https://github.com/prometheus/node_exporter/releases
NODE_VERSION="1.8.0"
cd /tmp
curl -fsSL "https://github.com/prometheus/node_exporter/releases/download/v${NODE_VERSION}/node_exporter-${NODE_VERSION}.linux-amd64.tar.gz" \
    -o node_exporter.tar.gz
tar -xzf node_exporter.tar.gz
sudo cp "node_exporter-${NODE_VERSION}.linux-amd64/node_exporter" /usr/local/bin/node_exporter
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /usr/local/bin/node_exporter
Troubleshoot this step:

Error seen: tar: node_exporter-X.Y.Z.linux-amd64: Cannot open: No such file or directory after extraction.
Recovery: The download likely failed silently â check file size with ls -lh /tmp/node_exporter.tar.gz; if it is under 1 MB the URL returned an error page. Verify the version at https://github.com/prometheus/node_exporter/releases and re-run the curl command.
Official docs: https://prometheus.io/docs/guides/node-exporter/
Step 1.3: Start All Services and Confirm Reachability
Write the Prometheus configuration file and systemd unit files for Prometheus, Node Exporter, and Grafana, then start all services. Confirmation: every service must report active (running) and each port must respond to a local HTTP probe before you proceed to Task 2.

# Prometheus scrape configuration â targets added in Task 2
sudo tee /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml <<'EOF'
global:
  scrape_interval:     15s
  evaluation_interval: 15s

rule_files:
  - /etc/prometheus/alert_rules.yml

alerting:
  alertmanagers:
    - static_configs:
        - targets: ["localhost:9093"]

scrape_configs:
  - job_name: prometheus
    static_configs:
      - targets: ["localhost:9090"]

  - job_name: node-exporter
    static_configs:
      - targets: ["localhost:9100"]

  - job_name: cloud-simulator
    static_configs:
      - targets: ["localhost:9106"]
EOF

sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml

# Create an empty alert rules file so Prometheus starts without errors
sudo tee /etc/prometheus/alert_rules.yml <<'EOF'
groups: []
EOF
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus/alert_rules.yml
# Prometheus systemd unit
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/prometheus.service <<'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=Prometheus
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
User=prometheus
Group=prometheus
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/prometheus \
  --config.file=/etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml \
  --storage.tsdb.path=/var/lib/prometheus \
  --web.console.templates=/etc/prometheus/consoles \
  --web.console.libraries=/etc/prometheus/console_libraries \
  --web.listen-address=0.0.0.0:9090 \
  --web.enable-lifecycle
Restart=on-failure

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

# Node Exporter systemd unit
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/node_exporter.service <<'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=Node Exporter
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
User=prometheus
Group=prometheus
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/node_exporter \
  --collector.systemd \
  --collector.processes
Restart=on-failure

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now prometheus node_exporter grafana-server
# Verify all three services are running
sudo systemctl is-active prometheus node_exporter grafana-server

# Probe each HTTP endpoint â all three must return HTTP 200
curl -fsSL -o /dev/null -w "Prometheus:  %{http_code}\n" http://localhost:9090/-/healthy
curl -fsSL -o /dev/null -w "NodeExporter:%{http_code}\n" http://localhost:9100/metrics
curl -fsSL -o /dev/null -w "Grafana:     %{http_code}\n" http://localhost:3000/api/health
Confirmation: all three lines print 200. If any service is not active, run sudo journalctl -u <service-name> -n 40 --no-pager to read the failure reason before continuing.

Task 2: Build the Cloud Metrics Simulator and Wire Grafana Dashboards
Step 2.1: Implement the Cloud Metrics Simulator
Design and deploy a Python HTTP server that exposes Prometheus-format metrics on port 9106, simulating the telemetry a real CloudWatch exporter would produce. The simulator must satisfy these constraints:

Expose a /metrics endpoint that returns valid Prometheus text exposition format (each line: metric_name{label="value"} numeric_value)
Emit at minimum the metric families listed in the interface contract below â label names and cardinality are your design decision
Run as a systemd service under the prometheus user so it restarts automatically on failure
Produce non-static values: each scrape must return freshly sampled random values within realistic operating ranges so time-series graphs show movement
# Interface contract â implement all methods; internals are your choice
class CloudMetricsSimulator:
    def get_ec2_metrics(self) -> list[str]:
        """
        Returns Prometheus-format lines for:
          aws_ec2_cpu_utilization    (0.0 â 100.0, percent)
          aws_ec2_memory_utilization (0.0 â 100.0, percent)
          aws_ec2_network_in_bytes   (positive float, bytes/s)
          aws_ec2_network_out_bytes  (positive float, bytes/s)
          aws_ec2_disk_utilization   (0.0 â 100.0, percent)
        Each metric must carry at minimum: instance_id, region labels.
        """
        ...

    def get_rds_metrics(self) -> list[str]:
        """
        Returns Prometheus-format lines for:
          aws_rds_cpu_utilization  (0.0 â 100.0, percent)
          aws_rds_connections      (integer, 0 â 500)
          aws_rds_free_storage_bytes (positive float, bytes)
        Each metric must carry at minimum: db_instance, engine labels.
        """
        ...

    def get_elb_metrics(self) -> list[str]:
        """
        Returns Prometheus-format lines for:
          aws_elb_request_count  (integer, requests per scrape interval)
          aws_elb_latency_seconds (0.001 â 5.0, seconds)
          aws_elb_healthy_hosts  (integer, 0 â 10)
        Each metric must carry at minimum: load_balancer, region labels.
        """
        ...

    def get_s3_metrics(self) -> list[str]:
        """
        Returns Prometheus-format lines for:
          aws_s3_bucket_size_bytes   (positive float, bytes)
          aws_s3_number_of_objects   (integer)
        Each metric must carry at minimum: bucket_name, region labels.
        """
        ...

    def render_all(self) -> str:
        """Concatenates all metric families into a single response body."""
        ...
