Task 3: Implement Alerting with Alertmanager
Step 3.1: Deploy Alertmanager and Define Alert Rules
Alertmanager (the component that receives firing alerts from Prometheus and routes them to receivers) must be installed as a systemd service on port 9093. Define Prometheus alerting rules that fire under the conditions in the contract below. The routing tree and receiver configuration are your design decision; at minimum configure one working receiver (a webhook receiver pointed at a local netcat listener is acceptable for lab validation).

# Alert rule contract â implement all rules in /etc/prometheus/alert_rules.yml
REQUIRED_ALERT_RULES: list[dict] = [
    {
        "alert":    "HighEC2CPU",
        "expr":     "aws_ec2_cpu_utilization > 85",
        "for":      "1m",
        "severity": "warning",
        "summary":  "EC2 instance CPU above 85% for 1 minute",
    },
    {
        "alert":    "HighEC2Memory",
        "expr":     "aws_ec2_memory_utilization > 80",
        "for":      "1m",
        "severity": "warning",
        "summary":  "EC2 instance memory above 80% for 1 minute",
    },
    {
        "alert":    "LowRDSStorage",
        "expr":     "aws_rds_free_storage_bytes < 2000000",
        "for":      "2m",
        "severity": "critical",
        "summary":  "RDS free storage below 2 MB for 2 minutes",
    },
    {
        "alert":    "ELBHighLatency",
        "expr":     "aws_elb_latency_seconds > 2",
        "for":      "1m",
        "severity": "warning",
        "summary":  "ELB latency above 2 seconds for 1 minute",
    },
    {
        "alert":    "TargetDown",
        "expr":     "up == 0",
        "for":      "30s",
        "severity": "critical",
        "summary":  "A monitored target has been unreachable for 30 seconds",
    },
]
After writing the rules file, validate it before reloading Prometheus:

# promtool exits non-zero and prints errors if any rule is malformed
sudo /usr/local/bin/promtool check rules /etc/prometheus/alert_rules.yml
Confirmation: promtool prints SUCCESS: X rules found with no errors.

Step 3.2: Configure Grafana Alerting and Verify End-to-End Alert Flow
Grafana's unified alerting system (introduced in Grafana 9) can independently evaluate alert rules against a data source and send notifications through its own contact points â this is separate from Prometheus/Alertmanager alerting. Configure both layers:

In Prometheus/Alertmanager: confirm at least one rule transitions to FIRING state by temporarily adjusting the simulator to emit a value that crosses a threshold, then restore normal ranges
In Grafana: create at least one Grafana-native alert rule on the aws_ec2_cpu_utilization metric with a contact point that logs to Grafana's alert history (no external email required)
To force a threshold crossing for testing, modify your simulator to emit a fixed value above the alert threshold for 90 seconds, then revert. Check alert state via the Prometheus API:

# List all currently active alerts â HighEC2CPU should appear while simulator emits > 85
curl -fsSL http://localhost:9090/api/v1/alerts \
    | python3 -c "
import sys, json
alerts = json.load(sys.stdin)['data']['alerts']
for a in alerts:
    print(a['labels']['alertname'], a['state'])
"
Confirmation: HighEC2CPU appears with state firing during the test window, then disappears after you restore normal simulator output. In Grafana, the alert history panel under Alerting shows at least one state transition event.

Expected Outcomes
Three live Grafana dashboards display continuously updating metrics from the cloud simulator and Node Exporter, with all panels showing data and no "No data" placeholders
At least one Prometheus alert rule transitions through pending to firing and back to inactive during the forced threshold test, confirming the full Prometheus -> Alertmanager -> Grafana alert pipeline is operational
Troubleshooting
If a Grafana panel shows "No data" despite the simulator running, what would you inspect in the Prometheus Targets page (http://localhost:9090/targets) and in the Grafana data source query inspector to determine whether the problem is a scrape failure, a label mismatch in your PromQL expression, or a data source misconfiguration?
If an alert rule stays in pending state indefinitely and never transitions to firing, what properties of the rule definition, the simulator's output range, and the Prometheus evaluation interval would you examine to identify whether the condition is never actually being met or whether the for duration is simply not elapsing?
Conclusion
This lab built a complete observability pipeline from an empty Ubuntu instance: a metrics simulator representing cloud services fed Prometheus, which backed Grafana dashboards and drove threshold-based alerts through Alertmanager. The architecture you assembled â exporter, time-series database, visualization layer, and alert router as independent services â mirrors the separation of concerns used in production monitoring stacks. Extending this to real AWS CloudWatch data requires only replacing the simulator with an authenticated CloudWatch exporter; every other component remains identical.
